cff-version: "1.2.0"
type: article
message: "If you use this work, please cite the paper below."

title: "Interpretive Kernel: From Occurrence Equivalence to Frame Equivalence"
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-07-24"

authors:
  - family-names: Case
    given-names: Denise M.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6165-7389"
    affiliation: "Northwest Missouri State University, School of Computer Science and Information Systems, Maryville, MO, USA"

repository-code: "https://github.com/structural-explainability/paper-220-interpretive-kernel"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"

abstract: >
  Many operational systems derive outputs by interpreting stable declarations
  against external frames that can change independently of those declarations.
  This paper formalizes when two frames count as equivalent relative to a finite
  probe family and a declared occurrence equivalence relation. The probe-indexed
  interpretation construction transports the occurrence equivalence relation to
  a frame equivalence relation by identifying two frame environments
  when every selected probe produces occurrence-equivalent outputs. The induced
  relation is the interpretive kernel. A reverse construction generates, from
  any declared frame regime, the least occurrence regime required to support it.
  The two maps form an adjunction between the lattices of occurrence and frame
  equivalence relations, yielding meet- and join-preservation laws and an exact
  least threshold. Pairwise thresholds provide an equivalence-relation-valued
  generalized pseudoultrametric representation of the kernel family. The
  framework is instantiated for recurring schedules using typed civil and
  resolved outputs, chronological canonicalization, finite observation horizons,
  and versioned frame environments. The result makes interpretive equivalence a
  declared, parameter-relative, and executable relation between rules of
  sameness on opposite sides of interpretation.

keywords:
  - interpretive kernels
  - occurrence equivalence
  - frame equivalence
  - observational equivalence
  - partition lattices
  - Galois connections
  - generalized ultrametrics
  - schedule semantics
  - time-zone data
  - structural explainability

